    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">USDA Choice Beef Explained</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Costco uses <strong>USDA Choice</strong> grade beef for their hot dogs. USDA beef grading is based on maturity and marbling (intramuscular fat):</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse border border-gray-300 text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-gray-100">
              <th class="border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2 text-left">Grade</th>
              <th class="border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2 text-left">Marbling</th>
              <th class="border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2 text-left">Common Use</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2 font-bold">USDA Prime</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2">Abundant</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2">High-end steaks</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-green-50 font-bold"><td class="border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2">USDA Choice ✓</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2">Moderate</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2">Hot dogs, ground beef, burgers</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2">USDA Select</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2">Slight</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2">Lean cuts, processed meats</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">USDA Choice is the second-highest grade and is widely considered excellent quality for processed meat products. It provides good flavor and tenderness without the premium price of Prime.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Why People Ask About Pork</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The question "is the Costco hot dog beef or pork?" is common for several reasons:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-4">
        <li><strong>Many hot dogs contain pork</strong> — Standard American hot dogs often use a mix of beef and pork, or pork alone. Brands like Oscar Mayer and Hebrew National have historically used pork blends.</li>
        <li><strong>Hebrew National was kosher-certified</strong> — Before 2009, Costco sold Hebrew National hot dogs (which were kosher). Kosher laws prohibit mixing meat and dairy but don't prohibit pork — however, kosher standards also require specific slaughter methods that most pork producers don't follow.</li>
        <li><strong>Religious dietary restrictions</strong> — Muslims (halal), Jews (kosher), and others need to know the meat type to determine if a product is permissible.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Regardless of the reason, the answer is clear: <strong>the Costco hot dog is 100% beef with zero pork content</strong>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
